Let $E \rightarrow$ Event of head showing up
$E_{1} \rightarrow$ Event of biased coin chosen
$E_{2} \rightarrow$ Event of unbiased coin chosen Now, $P\left(E_{2}\right)=\frac{1}{2}$ and $P\left(E_{1}\right)=\frac{1}{2}$
Also, $P\left(\frac{E}{E_{2}}\right)=\frac{1}{2}$ and $P\left(\frac{E}{E_{1}}\right)=\frac{3}{4}$
(by conditional probability) By Baye's theorem

\begin{aligned}
P\left(\frac{E_{2}}{E}\right)=& \frac{P\left(E_{2}\right) \cdot P\left(\frac{E}{E_{2}}\right)}{P\left(E_{2}\right) \cdot P\left(\frac{E}{E_{2}}\right)+P\left(E_{1}\right) \cdot P\left(\frac{E}{E_{1}}\right)} \\
&=\frac{\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{2}}{\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2} \times \frac{3}{4}}=\frac{2}{5}
\end{aligned}

